.TH PLACE 1
.SH NAME
place \- put parts together into an assembly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B place
[
.B \-s
.I meshdir
]
.I assembly.txt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Place
reads an assembly file
.RI ( assembly (5))
and writes the parts it names, renamed and placed, as one stream
.RI ( stream (5))
on the standard output: first the materials they use, once each, and
then each part with its place, its material and the materials of its
coats.
A part file is a stream of a folded part or of solids, the output of
.IR smfold (1)
or
.IR fillet (1),
or another assembly file, a subassembly, whose parts take names under
the name of its line, as front/knob/cap; a file that holds itself is
refused with the chain of the files.
The insertion path
.RB ( path )
and
.B with
are for the scene, and do not change the place, and the pictures go
only into the scene.
.PP
With
.BR \-s ,
.I place
writes instead a scene for
.IR view (1)
.RI ( scene (5)):
a mesh line for each part line, of the file
.IB meshdir / name .obj
(the mesh of the part's file in its own frame, as
.IR tomesh (1)
writes it), with the color of its material, the steps of its place,
and its insertion path, every turn with its point, or its
.BR with ;
and an image line for each picture.
A subassembly becomes a unit: a unit line with the steps and the tail
of its line, its own lines in its own frame and indented, their meshes
.IB meshdir / name /...,
and an end line.
.I Meshdir
is written as given, so it is relative to where the scene goes.
.SH SEE ALSO
interfere(1), view(1), assembly(5), scene(5), stream(5), sm(5)
